1. What is cp?

The cp command is used to copy files and directories from one location to another.

=> Practical Examples

Create a sample file:

echo "This is a sample file" > file1.txt

Copy the file:

cp file1.txt file2.txt

Check the copied file:

ls
cat file2.txt

Copy a file into another directory:

mkdir backup
cp file1.txt backup/

Copy a directory and its contents:
cp -r backup backup-copy

The -r option is required when copying directories.

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to copy files and directories while keeping the original files unchanged.

2. What is mv?

The mv command is used to move files and directories. It can also be used to rename them.

Practical Examples

Rename a file:

mv file1.txt renamed-file.txt

Move a file into a directory:

mv renamed-file.txt backup/

Rename a directory:

mv backup backup-files

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to move files and directories and how to rename them using the mv command.

3. What is SSH?

SSH stands for Secure Shell.

It is used to connect securely to a remote Linux server through the terminal.

=> Practical Examples

a) Connect to a remote Ubuntu server:

ssh ubuntu@SERVER_IP

b) Connect using a private key:

ssh -i ~/Downloads/testing.pem ubuntu@SERVER_IP

Example:

ssh -i ~/Downloads/testing.pem ubuntu@15.252.157.185

After connecting, I can run commands on the remote server.

Check the remote system:

hostname
pwd

Exit the remote server:

exit

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to connect to a remote Linux server securely using SSH and a private key.

4. What is SCP?

SCP stands for Secure Copy Protocol.

It is used to securely copy files between a local system and a remote server.

=> Practical Examples

a) Copy a local file to a remote server:

scp -i ~/Downloads/testing.pem app.conf ubuntu@SERVER_IP:/home/ubuntu/

Example:

scp -i ~/Downloads/testing.pem app.conf ubuntu@15.252.157.185:/home/ubuntu/

b) Copy a file from the remote server to the local system:

scp -i ~/Downloads/testing.pem ubuntu@SERVER_IP:/home/ubuntu/app.conf .

c) Copy a directory:

scp -r -i ~/Downloads/testing.pem my-folder

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to securely transfer files and directories between Ubuntu and an AWS EC2 server using SCP.

5. What is rsync?

The rsync command is used to copy and synchronize files and directories.

It is useful for backups because it copies only the files or changes that are needed.

mkdir -p source-folder
echo "Backup data" > source-folder/file.txt

Create a backup directory:

mkdir backup-folder

Synchronize the source directory:

rsync -av source-folder/ backup-folder/

Explanation:

-a means archive mode
-v means verbose output

Check the backup:

ls -R backup-folder

=> Install rsync
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y rsync

=> Dry Run

Before copying, we can check what will happen:

rsync -av --dry-run source-folder/ backup-folder/

The --dry-run option shows the changes without actually copying files.

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to synchronize files and directories using rsync. I also learned that it is useful for backups and transferring only changed files.
_____________________

1. In rsync, synchronize means make the destination folder match the source folder.

Simple example

Suppose your source folder contains:

source-folder/
├── file1.txt
└── file2.txt

You run:

rsync -av source-folder/ backup-folder/

Now the backup folder contains the same files:

backup-folder/
├── file1.txt
└── file2.txt

=> What happens when a file changes?

If you update file1.txt in the source folder:

echo "Updated data" >> source-folder/file1.txt

Run rsync again:

rsync -av source-folder/ backup-folder/

rsync checks the differences and copies the changed file to the backup folder.

It does not normally copy every file again. It copies only the files that need updating.

=> What happens when a new file is created?
touch source-folder/file3.txt

Run:

rsync -av source-folder/ backup-folder/

The new file3.txt is copied to the backup folder.

2. In the rsync command:

rsync -av source-folder/ backup-folder/

the -a flag means archive mode.

What does archive mode do?

Archive mode copies files and directories while preserving important information such as:

File permissions
Ownership
Timestamps
Symbolic links
Directory structure

It also copies directories recursively.
